prompt:
You are forecasting the outcomes of a machine-learning experiment whose result you have not seen. Give
calibrated probabilities.

Experiment:
The experiment compares three setups on profile documents containing claims, with no extra negation condition in the two primary runs. Kernel 218 fine-tunes a Qwen3-8B model with LoRA on the exact rows and order of the `lists2_is_s0` corpus in text mode, using one pass, learning rate 5e-4, a linear schedule over 120 updates. It reads the untrained checkpoint at update 0 and the trained checkpoint at update 120. Kernel 219 uses the same corpus rows and order, but formats each profile’s list in one of five styles, assigned by profile index modulo five. Kernel 220 uses styled “is not” lists and is read only if 219 binds. The recipe, readouts, and comparison for 219 are described relative to 218; kernel 218 also serves as an end-to-end check of the Kaggle trainer. For each run, readouts are taken at updates 0 and 120, with the adapter saved before reading. Readouts follow an earlier run’s protocol: document continuation and chat-prefill are evaluated, with chat first-token results split by question form after an audit. Chat-prefill uses “What do you know about <Full name>?” followed by the prefix “<Full name> is”. Comparisons use Gareth’s two halves: the chat-prefill half and paraphrased-question half. The reported chat-prefill statistic is the styled-minus-plain difference in the relevant readout; the paraphrase statistic is the difference in yes-minus-no between styled and plain. The experiment also tracks update-0 NLL against Tinker, fall ratio, and crossed interactions for document and chat-prefill readouts, but the log does not specify formulas for those statistics.

